            "text": "<h2></h2><p>Here's the entire business model. We're putting it on the internet for anyone to read because we have nothing to hide and honestly we could use the attention.</p><p><strong>You shop.</strong><br/>Real brands. Good products. Stuff you were going to buy anyway — candles, skincare, apparel, accessories, the jacket you've been eyeing for three weeks. We carry it. It's nice. You'll look great.</p><p><strong>We show you the math.</strong><br/>Every single product on our site tells you the exact dollar amount going to charity. Not a percentage buried in a footnote. Not a \"portion of proceeds\" which is corporate speak for \"we'll donate $11 and write a blog post about it.\" The actual number, right on the product page, before you buy. $42 to ALS Network. $24.80 to Step Up. $15.20 to Life Rolls On. You see it. It's real. We can't fake it because it's literally right there.</p><p><strong>We send the money.</strong><br/>Directly to the nonprofit. Real dollars. Real transfers. Then we talk about it on social media because we have absolutely no chill about this stuff. Transparency isn't a value for us — it's the whole point.</p><p><strong>We keep what's left.</strong><br/>After the product cost and the charitable donation, we keep a thin margin to pay for things like the website, shipping logistics, and the occasional coffee. Our margins are genuinely thin. Like, concerningly thin. Our accountant has mentioned this. Multiple times. We're aware.</p>",
            "width": "fit-content",
            "max_width": "none",
            "alignment": "center",
